---
import PageHero from '@src/components/pages/PageHero.astro';
import PageSection from '@src/components/pages/PageSection.astro';
import { routes } from '@src/data/routes';
import { site } from '@src/data/site';
import Base from '@src/layouts/Base.astro';
import { lastModified } from '@src/lib/dates';
import { routeSeo } from '@src/lib/seo/meta';

const updated = lastModified(...routes['/about'].sources);
---

<Base seo={routeSeo('/about')}>
  <PageHero
    kicker="about"
    title="Noticing when a person is doing a machine’s job."
    updated={updated}
  >
    <p>
      Most of what I have built started as somebody, usually me, doing the same thing by hand for
      the third time.
    </p>
  </PageHero>

  <PageSection id="story" title="The short version">
    <p>
      I was born in the United States, grew up in Pakistan, and came back at eighteen with a plan
      somebody else had made for me: start at a two-year school. Wilbur Wright College was close to
      an accident — it was nowhere near where I lived — and it turned out to be the best one I have
      had. A conversation in a physics class put me onto an engineering pathway, and two and a half
      years later I transferred to the University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign with a cohort of
      friends.
    </p>
    <p>
      Along the way I became the first computer science tutor Wright had, teaching calculus,
      physics, chemistry and two semesters of C. That is how Dr. Espiritu found me, and how I ended
      up on her research into matching mentors with mentees — students who had made that transfer
      helping students still trying to, and the question of whether an algorithm can see what makes
      a pairing work.
    </p>
    <p>
      I paid for all of it out of pocket: gas stations, busboy shifts, cleaning toilets, tutoring,
      lab assistant work. I mention it because it is the part of a résumé that a résumé cannot hold.
    </p>
  </PageSection>

  <PageSection id="work" title="What I do now" ground="body">
    <p>
      {site.jobTitle} at {site.employer.name} in Austin, which I moved to for the obvious reason. I
      sit with the client, find the step a person is doing by hand, and build whatever it takes to
      remove it — sometimes product work, sometimes the systems underneath.
      <a href="/experience">The experience page</a> has the roles in detail, and
      <a href="/#projects">the projects</a> are the work I set myself.
    </p>
    <p>
      Outside it I buy broken hardware and fix it. A lot I picked up last spring came with four
      Bambu Lab printers, which is how I accidentally acquired a small printing business, and how
      AMS-X exists: four spools is a hardware answer to a software problem, and standing at a
      printer to swap filament is exactly the kind of manual step worth deleting.
    </p>
    <p>I also watch an unreasonable amount of financial reality television.</p>
  </PageSection>
</Base>
